        "id": "bounded-history",
        "says": "An undo history has a maximum size and discards the oldest commands when it is full.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": "command-history",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "clear-redo",
        "says": "Executing a new command clears the redo stack, so redo never replays a history that no longer exists.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:3"
        ],
        "exercise": "command-history",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "command-log-replay",
        "says": "An append-only log of commands is an audit trail from which state can be rebuilt by replaying every command from the start.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:15",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:16"
        ],
        "exercise": "event-store",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "command-vs-strategy",
        "says": "A command binds a request to a specific receiver and may keep state for undo, while a strategy is a stateless algorithm applied to whatever data it is given.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:9",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:10"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "deferred-lambdas",
        "says": "When no undo is needed, a Runnable lambda is a command, and a queue of them defers execution to a later, first-in-first-out run.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:4"
        ],
        "exercise": "command-queue",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "invoker-decoupled",
        "says": "The invoker only knows the Command interface, never the concrete command or its receiver.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember"
        ],
        "exercise": "command-history",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "java-features",
        "says": "Records, sealed interfaces, virtual threads and pattern matching for switch give commands immutable data, closed hierarchies and cheap asynchronous execution.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Relevant Java 21 Features"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "incidental detail, not practised; the event-store practice already uses sealed records as commands"
      },
      {
        "id": "macro-reverse",
        "says": "A macro command executes its commands in order and undoes them in reverse order, and rejects an empty command list.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:13",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:14"
        ],
        "exercise": "macro-command",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "memento",
        "says": "For receivers with complex state, the Memento pattern saves and restores snapshots instead of reversing each operation.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a pointer to another pattern that the page does not show in code; the edit-commands practice covers saving undo state"
      },
      {
        "id": "mid-execution-failure",
        "says": "A command that fails partway can leave the receiver inconsistent, which calls for transaction-like rollback.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the page only names the concern; rollback is practised with real transactions in module 45, and event-store grades that a failed command is not logged"
      },
      {
        "id": "not-functional",
        "says": "A Command with execute(), undo() and description() is not a functional interface, so a lambda cannot be passed where one is expected.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:11",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:12"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "request-as-object",
        "says": "A command object holds its receiver and parameters and knows how to execute and undo the request it stands for.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Concept Explanation",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:6"
        ],
        "exercise": "edit-commands",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-receiver-races",
        "says": "Commands that share a receiver and run concurrently race unless they run sequentially or the receiver is thread-safe.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the page gives no code for it and races on a receiver are practised in the concurrency modules"
      },
      {
        "id": "state-at-execute",
        "says": "A command saves the state its undo needs when execute() runs, not when it is constructed.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:1"
        ],
        "exercise": "edit-commands",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "two-stacks",
        "says": "An invoker implements undo and redo with two stacks, moving a command from one to the other, and reports an empty stack instead of failing.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:7",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:8"
        ],
        "exercise": "command-history",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "undo-guard",
        "says": "Undoing a command that never executed throws IllegalStateException instead of corrupting the receiver.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them",
          "example:38-behavioral-patterns/README_9.3.3.md:2"
        ],
        "exercise": "edit-commands",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "version-history",
        "says": "Lambdas and CompletableFuture came in Java 8, records in 16, sealed interfaces in 17 and virtual threads in 21.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Evolution Across Java Versions"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "incidental detail, not practised"
